import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
const content = "\u003cp>If these two names are already on your shortlist, you are not hunting for a generic Filipino dating directory. You are trying to decide whether an international, general-purpose site or a Christian-positioned one matches how you want to meet someone, what you can actually do before you pay, and how each company describes safety, billing and exit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>We write this as FilWest. We build a project around \u003ca href=\"https://filipinawest.com/\">Filipino-Western relationships\u003c/a>, and this page is our comparison of public materials, not an independent lab test, ranking or endorsement. We checked the public homepages, help pages and terms on 8 September 2026. We did not create personal accounts, buy membership, run checkout, or verify live messaging, video or safety tools. Treat every feature claim below as what those sites publish. If a marketing page and the terms disagree about what you would be buying, pause and ask the company to clarify before you authorise a charge.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-each-site-says-it-is-for\">Who Each Site Says It Is For\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"https://www.filipinocupid.com/\">FilipinoCupid\u003c/a> presents itself as an international dating site for Filipino singles and people seeking relationships with them. Its public home page frames a large, worldwide membership and a Cupid Media network of niche sites. That positioning matters if you want a broad catalogue and you are comfortable searching across many relationship styles rather than a faith filter.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Christian Filipina emphasises Christian values and compatible friendships as a basis for relationships. Its public home copy presents itself as a place for Christian singles seeking faith-based connection, and it describes relationship specialists and a screening process for new applications. That is a different product story: shared belief and friendship first, then romance, rather than a general international pool.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Neither public description tells you who will actually reply, how devout a given member is, or whether a profile is current. Positioning tells you the advertised premise, not the person on the other end. If church, shared faith language and a values-led community are non-negotiable, Christian Filipina is the site whose own words match that job. If you want Filipino dating without a Christian frame, FilipinoCupid is the site that says it is built for that wider search. Paying on the site whose premise you do not share means you will be sorting against people who joined for a different reason. That is a fit trade-off. It is not evidence that a first billing cycle will be wasted, and it is not a reason to buy both in the hope the pool will later match you.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>We are not a third brand in that faith-versus-international fork. We are a Filipino-Western dating project writing about two incumbents you are already comparing. Use their own positioning as the first cut, then look at how conversation and money actually work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"free-registration-does-not-mean-you-can-talk-freely\">Free Registration Does Not Mean You Can Talk Freely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Both sites let you register without paying. The decision is what that free account can send, and what stays locked until someone upgrades.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>FilipinoCupid offers free membership with limited features and charges for access to additional features. \u003ca href=\"https://www.filipinocupid.com/en/general/termsofuse\">FilipinoCupid’s membership terms\u003c/a> also describe paid subscriber tiers and coins used for paid extras such as virtual gifts. That is a familiar Cupid Media pattern: browse and use some tools on free registration, then pay when you want the remaining communication and matching tools. FilipinoCupid states that paid services renew automatically unless you opt out. Before you tap upgrade, read the period, the price on that screen, the currency, and how to turn auto-renewal off in the help section it points to. Auto-renewal is not a minor footnote. It is how the next charge happens if you do nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Christian Filipina’s messaging model is easy to oversimplify, and the public pages do not all describe it the same way. Christian Filipina says two free members can exchange pre-written messages, while a free member can send a custom reply to an upgraded member. That is the access detail that changes behaviour: you can test the waters with stock lines, and a custom reply becomes possible when at least one person has paid. \u003ca href=\"https://www.christianfilipina.com/faq\">Christian Filipina’s FAQ\u003c/a> is where that free-level and custom-reply distinction is spelled out, including the point that both people do not need to upgrade for a custom exchange to exist once one of them has.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ca href=\"https://www.christianfilipina.com/terms_of_use.html\">Christian Filipina’s terms\u003c/a> put a harder edge on initiation. They say you may register free, and that to use the services to initiate communication with other members you must become a paid member. Read those two documents together rather than picking the softer sentence. Pre-written exchanges and a custom reply to someone who already paid are not the same as unlimited original outreach from a free account. Paid initiation, custom replies and free registration answer different questions. If you need to write your own first message to people who have not paid, assume you are looking at an upgrade, then confirm that on the live membership page.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Christian Filipina also describes optional site credits, but says messages unlocked with credits cannot contain contact details. Credits buy extra on-site features. They are not a substitute for a full upgrade if the point of paying is to exchange email or a phone number. The same terms say credits have no cash value and purchased credits are non-refundable. If a checkout offers credits instead of (or on top of) a membership, ask whether you are buying conversation inside the site or the right to move off it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Exact daily quotas, cooldowns and current prices were not established in this public check. Do not plan a month of outreach from a screenshot or from memory of another Cupid Media site.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"commitment-cancellation-and-refunds-are-separate-switches\">Commitment, Cancellation And Refunds Are Separate Switches\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Once you pay, the next decision is not “do I like the site.” It is how the contract ends.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Christian Filipina states that a committed payment plan cannot be cancelled to avoid the remaining payments. That is not the same as a subscription you can stop after the current period. If the upgrade path offers a discounted series of instalments, you are agreeing to the series, not to a single month you can abandon when the replies disappoint you.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Christian Filipina directs members cancelling auto-renewal after the initial membership period to its online interface or telephone support. A committed first stretch and later auto-renewal can sit on the same membership, so do not treat those as alternative products you pick between. If remaining committed payments are still due, cancelling auto-renewal will not wipe them. After the initial membership period, use the online interface or telephone support the terms name when you want renewal to stop. Christian Filipina says cancelling membership does not automatically qualify you for a refund. Stopping future charges and recovering money already taken are two requests. The terms also describe cases where using the site during an initial period, including logging in or sending or receiving messages and winks, can remove refund eligibility. Read that before you treat a first login as harmless.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>FilipinoCupid’s refund language is also conditional. \u003ca href=\"https://www.filipinocupid.com/en/general/paymentrefund\">FilipinoCupid’s refund policy\u003c/a> describes full refunds in listed situations such as serious technical problems, a request made before any contact with members, and other cases under the terms of use. It also says there are no partial refunds for unused time unless you have not started using the service and have not initiated or received contact, plus other cases in those terms. Some US and UK consumer clauses in the terms add extra cancellation windows for certain residents. That is not a universal no-refund rule, and it is not legal advice. It is a reason to match your country, your start date and whether you already sent or opened mail before you assume a chargeback is your first move.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>On any of these sites, including ours when a paid plan exists, the practical questions before purchase are the same: what amount will be charged, in which currency, through which billing channel (card on the site, phone support, or an app store), and whether the plan auto-renews, runs as a committed payment plan, or ends when the period ends. Public marketing and public terms can disagree, including on coins and gifts. If the pages you read do not match the charge you are asked to authorise, pause and ask the company to clarify in writing before you pay. Do not settle an unclear contract by tapping the button that happens to be in front of you.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safety-copy-is-advice-not-a-score\">Safety Copy Is Advice, Not A Score\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Fit and access still leave the safety question: what each company says it does, and what it openly does not promise.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Christian Filipina describes profile verification as optional. Its FAQ outlines levels that can include a government-issued ID, and in higher levels additional documents such as a social-security number and a pastor’s letter. Optional means you may see unverified profiles, and a badge is not a criminal record check. The same FAQ states that the company does not conduct criminal background screenings, that identity theft can produce a false profile, and that caution with strangers is still required.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>FilipinoCupid’s terms take a similar line on identity. They say the company does not currently undertake routine background checks or verify information as a matter of course, while reserving the right to investigate. They also say the company cannot warrant that each member is who they claim to be. Its public dating-safety page tells you to keep early chat on the platform, treat money requests and refusal of a call or meeting as warnings, and report solicitation.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>None of that lets us call one site safe and the other unsafe, or say that either site lacks verification. Both publish optional or on-request identity steps and both publish member-responsibility language. Official safety pages are instructions, not evidence that scams never happen or that a review team catches every fake. Anyone who can steal an identity can fill in a form. Someone who pushes you off the site, especially while asking for money, is a reason to slow down. That does not mean every money mention or every request to change channel is a scheme. Your job is the same on both sites: stay on-platform until you have a reason to trust the person, refuse wire transfers and gift cards you have not agreed after meeting, and use the report tools they describe.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If a verified badge matters to you, look for how that site defines it, whether it is optional, and what it does not cover. Keep your own \u003ca href=\"https://filipinawest.com/safety/\">dating safety\u003c/a> habits in parallel either way: identity language on a help page is not a substitute for how you handle money, travel and first meetings.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-we-sit-on-this-shortlist\">Where We Sit On This Shortlist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You should know why this comparison exists on our site. We are not a review magazine. We are FilWest, and we are explaining two brands Western men already put side by side when they want a Filipino-Western match.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Our free offer is for women from the Philippines. If you are a Western man comparing these two names, that offer does not describe your access on our project. If you later look at our plans, ask the same billing questions you should ask FilipinoCupid and Christian Filipina: amount, currency, channel and renewal. If our marketing and our terms ever disagree on coins, gifts or what a plan includes, stop and get a clear answer before you pay.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Choose us only if a Filipino-Western dating project is the search you actually want, rather than a Christian-specialist site or a general international Cupid Media site. Choose one of the two sites in this article if their published fit and access model is the one you are willing to accept for the next billing cycle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-do-with-the-comparison\">What To Do With The Comparison\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pick the published premise you want to search under, then accept the access and exit rules that come with it. FilipinoCupid is the international, general Filipino dating option with limited free tools, paid extras and auto-renewal you must opt out of. Christian Filipina is the faith-and-friendship option, with pre-written free exchanges, custom replies once someone has upgraded, paid initiation in the terms, optional site credits that still cannot carry contact details, and a committed payment plan that does not let you skip remaining instalments. Cancellation is not a refund on either.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>If shared Christian practice is the point of the search, start on Christian Filipina and read the FAQ and terms until the messaging and payment model is clear. If you want a wider international Filipino site and you accept a free-plus-upgrade ladder with auto-renewal, start on FilipinoCupid and read the membership terms and refund policy before the first paid click. If you want a Filipino-Western project that is ours, use the home page linked above to see who we are for, and use the safety page for the checklist that still applies after you pick a site. When copy and contract do not match, get the charge, currency, billing channel and renewal explained before you authorise payment.\u003c/p>";
